2. Next, we will create a subquery to filter out the on-time flights based on the condition that the actual departure time is equal to the scheduled departure time. This will help us focus only on the relevant data for each airline carrier.

```sql
WITH on_time_flights AS (
    SELECT source_airline_carrier, act_dep_time FROM your_table WHERE act_dep_time = scheduled_dep_time
)
SELECT source_airline_carrier, COUNT(*) as num_on_time_flights
FROM on_time_flights
GROUP BY source_airline_carrier;
```

3. Finally, we will combine the sorted data and the count of on-time flights for each airline carrier in a single query using JOIN. This will provide us with the desired result: grouped flights by source airline carrier and the number of on-time arrivals.

```sql
SELECT a.source_airline_carrier, COUNT(a.act_dep_time) as num_on_time_flights, b.num_on_time_flights
FROM (
    SELECT source_airline_carrier, act_dep_time FROM your_table ORDER BY source_airline_carrier, act_dep_time
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    WITH on_time_flights AS (
        SELECT source_airline_carrier, act_dep_time FROM your_table WHERE act_dep_time = scheduled_dep_time
    )
    SELECT source_airline_carrier, COUNT(*) as num_on_time_flights
    FROM on_time_flights
    GROUP BY source_airline_carrier
) b ON a.source_airline_carrier = b.source_airline_carrier
GROUP BY a.source_airline_carrier;
```
